# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Secrets and credentials — nothing here may ever reach Git.
#
# This is a backstop, not the control. The real controls are: Workload Identity
# (so no GCP key exists to leak), CI secret stores, and a gitleaks scan before
# submitting. A .gitignore only helps for files you remembered to name.
# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
*.pem
*.key
*.p12
*.pfx
*-key.json
*credentials*.json
*service-account*.json
.env
.env.*
!.env.example

# kubeconfigs: a cluster admin credential in a file people copy around
kubeconfig
kubeconfig-*
.kube/
*.kubeconfig

# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Terraform
# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
.terraform/
.terraform.tfstate.lock.info

# State is committed nowhere — it lives in the GCS backend. Ignored here so a
# stray local run (e.g. -backend=false experiments) cannot commit one; state
# holds the cluster CA and endpoint.
*.tfstate
*.tfstate.*

# tfvars holds the project id and the operator's home IP. The .example is
# committed; the real file is not.
*.tfvars
*.tfvars.json
!*.tfvars.example

# Plan files are readable archives of the whole configuration and its values.
*.tfplan
# `*.tfplan` alone does NOT match a file literally named `tfplan`, which is what
# `terraform plan -out=tfplan` writes — the name the README and the Terraform CI
# workflow both use. The pattern looked like it covered the case and did not, so
# the plan file reached this repository twice before anyone noticed.
#
# A plan archive is not an innocent artifact: it embeds a FULL tfstate snapshot
# plus every variable value. Nothing about it is plaintext-searchable, because the
# zip entries are compressed — which is why gitleaks and GitHub secret scanning
# both stayed quiet. No credential leaked, and that is Workload Identity doing
# its job: there is no key anywhere to leak.
tfplan
tfplan.*
crash.log
crash.*.log

# Any captured Terraform output, e.g. `| Tee-Object -FilePath apply.log` so a
# silent failure leaves evidence. Useful locally, never a repository artifact.
#
# This entry exists because apply.log was committed to a public repo twice inside
# an hour, both times by `git add -A`, the second time in the very commit that
# claimed to be removing it. A guard checking `'*.log' not in gitignore` passed
# over `crash.*.log` above and silently skipped the edit — a substring test
# colliding with unrelated content.
*.log

# .terraform.lock.hcl IS committed on purpose (note the negation): it pins
# provider versions and checksums, which is what makes builds reproducible.
!.terraform.lock.hcl

# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Go
# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
/app/app
/app/hello-api
*.exe
*.test
*.out
coverage.*
vendor/

# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Ansible
# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
*.retry
ansible/inventory/hosts.local.ini
ansible/vault-password
.vault_pass

# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Editors / OS
# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
.idea/
.vscode/
!.vscode/extensions.json
*.swp
*~
.DS_Store
Thumbs.db
desktop.ini

# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Local scratch
# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
/tmp/
/scratch/
*.local.yaml
*.local.yml
